# 3. Reruns observe and adopt landed effects; no persisted execution state

Status: Accepted (implemented in #124)

## Context

- A release is many external effects (registry publishes, tags, GitHub releases, asset uploads). Any run can die partway.
- Owner requirements: a merged but unpublished version must be recoverable without inventing a newer version; execution must fail unless every required operation reaches a verified terminal-success state; partial publication must be safely resumable from provider state (incident report §2, requirements 4, 7, 8).
- The same report proposed durably persisted execution state (§8, SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-TERMINALITY criterion 3).

## Decision

Every run observes each operation's provider state immediately before that operation's effect and adopts an effect that already landed exactly as planned (a registry version already published is adopted with a warning); anything other than exact or absent stops the run (`execute_release`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_execution.rs`). Execution state lives in memory for one run. A receipt is written only when every planned operation reached verified success; an incomplete run exits non-zero. An older merged release is recovered by a fresh run that plans it with `release plan --from-release-commit <sha> --decision <path> --orchestration-revision <current sha>`, not from a state file. Callisto's own release workflow exposes this as a `release_source_sha` dispatch input; workflows generated by `callisto init` do not (`crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/init.rs` tests).

## Options considered

- **Persisted execution state file with resume** — shipped in #39, proposed again in incident report §8, removed in #124: "CI never reads execution state back (every run is a fresh runner), yet an Initial run turned an already-published registry version into E174, so 'Re-run failed jobs' could never finish a partial release" (#124).
- **Recovery commands (`release reconcile`, `release execute --recovery`, `--state`) and Initial/Recovery run kinds** — rejected with the state file in #124. Removed with them: the workspace lock, `AdoptedExact`/`RecoveredExact`, E173, E174 and the release-state schema.
- **Rerun the historical workflow run to recover** — rejected. A rerun of an old GitHub Actions run does not pick up workflow fixes merged later (incident report §2 requirement 6; §6 F-003).
- **Post-publish re-observation pass to build the receipt** — rejected: a flaky registry read could fail a release whose effects had all landed (#122, #124).

## Consequences

- A rerun ("Re-run failed jobs" or a fresh run on the release commit) finishes a partial release whose remaining operations can now succeed; "Re-run failed jobs" does not pick up workflow fixes merged later.
- A registry publish that is complete once its version is visible (npm) is issued and confirmed in two steps within one run: the executor issues every publish it can, lets another npm publish follow an issued one, and confirms the issued publishes together before anything is announced. The issued set lives in memory like the rest of the run; a crash between issue and confirmation leaves the operation unproven, and the rerun observes it first, as for any other effect.
- Adoption depends on precise observation. An effect that landed differently (for example a conflicting existing tag) must fail closed, never be adopted.
- No local record of a failed run survives it; the providers are the record.
- Tag identity follows [ADR 9](0009-tag-identity-is-commit-and-annotation.md). A Python release is complete when every built file is on the index, not when the version is visible (E306, E307).

## Enforcement

- `crates/callisto-cli/tests/release_hardening_tests.rs`: `p01_rerun_after_a_failed_publish_publishes_and_issues_a_receipt`, `p02_rerun_after_a_crate_is_already_published_completes_and_issues_a_receipt`, `p14_rerun_with_a_conflicting_existing_tag_fails_closed`.
- `crates/callisto-cli/tests/release_command_e2e_tests.rs`: `a_failed_run_writes_no_receipt_and_a_rerun_completes`.
- `crates/callisto-cli/tests/durable_release_e2e_tests.rs`: `newer_coordinator_executes_and_reruns_an_older_release_source`; rerun scenarios in `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_simulator.rs`.
- `ReleaseExecutionStateV1` (`crates/callisto-model/src/release/execution.rs`) has no wire shape.

## Revisit when

- Release execution moves to an environment that keeps state between runs, and a provider cannot be observed well enough to adopt its effects.
- A provider offers no reliable way to tell "already landed as planned" from "landed differently".

## Sources

- PR #124 (592396564), commit bodies and PR body; closed PR #122
- PR #101 (279e32361): "reject incomplete quiescent execution", "accept an explicit recovery source"
- PR #39 (eeda4a2f5): durable attested execution
- Incident report §2, §6 (F-003), §8 (`git show 11038b11b^:.claude/plans/RELEASE-SELF-HOSTING-INCIDENT-2026-09-16.md`)
# 9. A tag's identity is its commit and being annotated

Status: Accepted (implemented in #154)

## Context

- A rerun adopts effects that already landed (ADR 3), so it must decide whether an existing tag is the one it planned.
- The local check read the tag with `git for-each-ref` and compared the target commit and the annotation text. The remote check uses `git ls-remote`, which returns the tag object and peeled commit but not the annotation text, so it compared only the commit.
- The same repository state could therefore be adopted or rejected depending on whether the remote tag had been fetched.

## Decision

An existing tag is the planned tag when it names the planned commit and is annotated. Annotation text is not compared, locally or remotely. A lightweight tag or a tag on another commit is a conflict (E167) and is never adopted.

## Options considered

- **Compare the annotation text on both sides** — rejected: the remote side needs the tag object fetched first, and a tag created by a person or an earlier template would block every rerun.
- **Keep the split (text locally, commit remotely)** — rejected: the outcome depends on fetch state.

## Consequences

- Local and remote checks agree.
- A tag with a different message is adopted. The receipt records only the peeled commit (`ProviderEvidenceV1::GitTag { peeled_commit }` in `crates/callisto-model/src/release_observation.rs`), and `gh release create --verify-tag` checks only that the tag exists.

## Enforcement

- Tests in `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/provider/tag.rs` for local and remote observation (different annotation text is adopted; lightweight and other-commit tags conflict).
- REL-RERUN-02 and REL-RERUN-03 in `docs/specs/release.json`.

## Revisit when

- Something downstream starts reading the annotation text (release notes, signing policy, provenance).

## Sources

- Owner decision, `git show 07154021c:docs/projects/ROAD-TO-V1.md` "v1 fix plan" decisions (2026-09-25)
- The fix in this PR: "fix: stop comparing tag annotation text in release rerun observation"
//! The commit that set a package's version, so a rerun after a half-finished release cannot tag later code.

use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

use callisto_manifests::{read_effective_version, WorkspaceReads};
use callisto_model::vcs::{GitAccess, VcsError};
use callisto_model::{ManifestDecl, ManifestError, ReleaseDecisionV1, ReleasePackageId, Version};

use crate::changed::package_paths;
use crate::{DependencyResolver, GraphError, Workspace};

/// A package whose files differ from the commit that set the version it is about to release, or whose version
/// commit could not be found.
pub(super) struct MovedPast {
    pub package: ReleasePackageId,
    pub version: String,
    /// `None` when no commit on the first-parent history sets this version.
    pub version_commit: Option<String>,
}

/// The selected packages that differ from the commit that set their release version, or whose version commit is
/// unknown. `resumable` packages are tagged on HEAD already.
///
/// A package is compared by tree content, so a revert or a change made only inside a merge commit counts the same as
/// a plain commit. The caller refuses only the ones whose version the registry already holds.
pub(super) fn packages_moved_past_their_version<R: callisto_model::CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver>(
    workspace: &Workspace<'_, R, D>,
    decision: &ReleaseDecisionV1,
    resumable: &BTreeSet<ReleasePackageId>,
) -> Result<Vec<MovedPast>, GraphError> {
    let git = workspace.git_access();
    let mut moved = Vec::new();
    for package in workspace.graph.packages() {
        let ids = crate::commands::release_decision::release_package_ids(&workspace.identity, package)?;
        let Some(entry) = decision
            .entries
            .iter()
            .find(|entry| ids.contains(&entry.package) && !resumable.contains(&entry.package))
        else {
            continue;
        };
        let Some(manifest) = package.canonical_manifests().next() else {
            continue;
        };
        // A manifest that is not committed has no history to compare; the clean-tree rule covers it.
        let Some(head) = effective_version_at(git, "HEAD", manifest)? else {
            continue;
        };
        let version_commit = commit_setting_version(git, manifest, &head, &entry.target_version)?;
        let unchanged = match &version_commit {
            Some(commit) => git.same_content_at(commit, "HEAD", &package_paths(package))?,
            None => false,
        };
        if !unchanged {
            moved.push(MovedPast {
                package: entry.package.clone(),
                version: entry.target_version.render().to_owned(),
                version_commit,
            });
        }
    }
    Ok(moved)
}

/// The newest first-parent commit whose manifest holds `version` while its parent's does not.
///
/// Only the manifest and, for an inherited version, the workspace manifest that declares it can set the version, so
/// those are the only paths walked. A history with no such commit gives `None`, never a guess.
fn commit_setting_version(
    git: &GitAccess<'_>,
    manifest: &ManifestDecl,
    head: &callisto_manifests::EffectiveManifestVersion,
    version: &Version,
) -> Result<Option<String>, GraphError> {
    let mut paths = vec![manifest.path.clone()];
    if head.authority != manifest.path {
        paths.push(head.authority.clone());
    }
    for commit in git.first_parent_commits_touching(&paths)? {
        if effective_version_at(git, &commit, manifest)?
            .map(|found| found.version)
            .as_ref()
            != Some(version)
        {
            continue;
        }
        // A root commit has no parent, so the version was set there.
        let parent = effective_version_at(git, &format!("{commit}^"), manifest)?;
        if parent.map(|found| found.version).as_ref() != Some(version) {
            return Ok(Some(commit));
        }
    }
    Ok(None)
}

/// The version `manifest` holds at `rev`, resolving a workspace-inherited one. A manifest that is absent, does not
/// parse or declares no version there gives `None`; a failing git read is an error.
fn effective_version_at(
    git: &GitAccess<'_>,
    rev: &str,
    manifest: &ManifestDecl,
) -> Result<Option<callisto_manifests::EffectiveManifestVersion>, GraphError> {
    // `read_effective_version` reports manifest errors only, so a git failure is held here and returned after.
    let git_failure: RefCell<Option<VcsError>> = RefCell::new(None);
    let mut read = |path: &std::path::Path| -> Result<Option<String>, ManifestError> {
        Ok(git.read_at(rev, path).unwrap_or_else(|error| {
            git_failure.borrow_mut().get_or_insert(error);
            None
        }))
    };
    let found = match read(&manifest.path)? {
        Some(source) => read_effective_version(
            manifest.format,
            &source,
            &manifest.path,
            &mut WorkspaceReads::default(),
            &mut read,
        )
        .ok()
        .flatten(),
        None => None,
    };
    match git_failure.into_inner() {
        Some(error) => Err(error.into()),
        None => Ok(found),
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use std::path::Path;

    use super::*;
    use crate::commands::release::tests::{fixture, RealGitRunner};
    use crate::IgnoreWalkLocator;

    fn git(root: &Path, args: &[&str]) -> String {
        let output = std::process::Command::new("git")
            .args(args)
            .current_dir(root)
            .output()
            .unwrap();
        assert!(output.status.success(), "git {args:?}");
        String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout).trim().to_owned()
    }

    fn commit_all(root: &Path, message: &str) -> String {
        git(root, &["add", "-A"]);
        git(root, &["commit", "-q", "-m", message]);
        git(root, &["rev-parse", "HEAD"])
    }

    fn moved(root: &Path) -> Vec<(String, Option<String>)> {
        let workspace = Workspace::load(root.to_path_buf(), &IgnoreWalkLocator::new(root), &RealGitRunner).unwrap();
        let decision = crate::commands::release_decision::derive_release_roster(&workspace, &[], &BTreeSet::new())
            .unwrap()
            .expect("unreleased packages");
        packages_moved_past_their_version(&workspace, &decision, &BTreeSet::new())
            .unwrap()
            .into_iter()
            .map(|moved| (moved.package.to_string(), moved.version_commit))
            .collect()
    }

    /// A workspace whose members `a` and `b` inherit `1.0.0` from the root, committed once.
    fn inheriting_workspace(root: &Path) -> String {
        std::fs::write(
            root.join("Cargo.toml"),
            "[workspace]\nmembers = [\"crates/*\"]\nresolver = \"3\"\n\n[workspace.package]\nversion = \"1.0.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n",
        )
        .unwrap();
        for name in ["a", "b"] {
            std::fs::create_dir_all(root.join(format!("crates/{name}/src"))).unwrap();
            std::fs::write(
                root.join(format!("crates/{name}/Cargo.toml")),
                format!("[package]\nname = \"{name}\"\nversion.workspace = true\nedition.workspace = true\n"),
            )
            .unwrap();
            std::fs::write(root.join(format!("crates/{name}/src/lib.rs")), "\n").unwrap();
        }
        std::fs::write(root.join("callisto.toml"), "").unwrap();
        commit_all(root, "workspace")
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_package_untouched_since_its_version_commit_is_not_reported() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        assert!(moved(dir.path()).is_empty());
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_later_commit_in_the_package_is_reported_with_the_version_commit() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        let version_commit = git(dir.path(), &["rev-parse", "HEAD"]);
        std::fs::write(dir.path().join("NOTES.md"), "later\n").unwrap();
        commit_all(dir.path(), "later");
        assert_eq!(
            moved(dir.path()),
            vec![("cargo/release-fixture".to_owned(), Some(version_commit))]
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_commit_in_another_package_is_not_a_move() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        let root = dir.path();
        let version_commit = inheriting_workspace(root);
        std::fs::write(root.join("crates/b/src/lib.rs"), "pub fn b() {}\n").unwrap();
        commit_all(root, "change b");
        assert_eq!(moved(root), vec![("cargo/b".to_owned(), Some(version_commit))]);
    }

    #[test]
    fn an_inherited_version_is_located_at_the_workspace_manifest_that_set_it() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        let root = dir.path();
        let version_commit = inheriting_workspace(root);
        std::fs::write(root.join("crates/a/src/lib.rs"), "pub fn a() {}\n").unwrap();
        commit_all(root, "change a");
        assert_eq!(
            moved(root),
            vec![("cargo/a".to_owned(), Some(version_commit))],
            "a package that inherits its version is checked like any other"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_version_set_long_ago_is_still_found() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        let root = dir.path();
        let version_commit = git(root, &["rev-parse", "HEAD"]);
        let manifest = root.join("Cargo.toml");
        let original = std::fs::read_to_string(&manifest).unwrap();
        for index in 0..105 {
            std::fs::write(&manifest, format!("{original}\n# edit {index}\n")).unwrap();
            commit_all(root, "touch the manifest");
        }
        assert_eq!(
            moved(root),
            vec![("cargo/release-fixture".to_owned(), Some(version_commit))],
            "more than a hundred manifest edits above the version commit must not hide it"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_release_branch_merged_after_unrelated_trunk_commits_is_not_a_move() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        let root = dir.path();
        git(root, &["switch", "-q", "-c", "trunk"]);
        git(root, &["switch", "-q", "-c", "release"]);
        let manifest = root.join("Cargo.toml");
        let text = std::fs::read_to_string(&manifest).unwrap();
        std::fs::write(&manifest, text.replace("1.2.3", "1.3.0")).unwrap();
        commit_all(root, "release 1.3.0");
        git(root, &["switch", "-q", "trunk"]);
        std::fs::write(root.join("NOTES.md"), "trunk moved on\n").unwrap();
        commit_all(root, "trunk moved on");
        git(root, &["merge", "-q", "--no-ff", "-m", "merge the release", "release"]);
        assert!(
            moved(root).is_empty(),
            "HEAD is the merge that landed the version, so nothing differs from the commit that set it"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_version_no_commit_set_is_reported_without_a_commit() {
        let (dir, _) = fixture();
        let root = dir.path();
        let manifest = root.join("Cargo.toml");
        let text = std::fs::read_to_string(&manifest).unwrap();
        let bumped = text.replacen("version = \"", "version = \"9", 1);
        std::fs::write(&manifest, bumped).unwrap();
        // Uncommitted: no commit holds this version, so the guard cannot say it is unchanged.
        assert!(moved(root).iter().all(|(_, commit)| commit.is_none()));
    }
}

